DRIFT="No set comes close to 0.5, so all models must extrapolate. Dropping absolute price columns lowers the AUC from 0.953 to 0.921, and LP columns alone give 0.869. A first version with calendar columns gave impossible results (a superset scoring below its subset), which exposed the memorisation. Drift decided nothing by itself: the net kept the absolute columns because validation favoured them."
PERM="The hypothesis held. Shuffling the LP schedule costs about 0.24 in both models, far above any other group, followed by LP sensitivity (about 0.02). Market columns cost the net 0.0087 against 0.0005 for the trees. Not predicted: the net loses 0.082 on the unit group and 0.065 on `unit_index` alone, despite having a unit embedding. I do not know why. The blend loses only 0.0018 on that column. Single features cost at most 0.002 in the blend, since LP columns can replace each other, so read the bars together with the group table. They use one shuffle and one seed, and differences below about 0.0002 are not meaningful."
ERROR="- **Units:** learning adds most where the LP is weakest: Vinje G3 0.929 to 0.976, Hogga 0.938 to 0.982, Kjela 0.950 to 0.989. These remain the weakest. Byrte (0.9995) and Tokke (about 0.996) are nearly solved. The blend matches or beats both members on all 14 units, though their logits correlate at 0.97.\n# - **Unit and year:** weakest cells are Lio 2022 (0.964), Vinje G3 2020 (0.964), Hogga 2017 (0.968) and Haukeli 2022 (0.974). The weakest year is 2018 (0.99037). 2020, by far the weakest in phase 1 (E021a 0.97653), is now 0.99214.\n# - **Price and month:** nearly flat across price octiles (0.992-0.996). April is weakest for both blend and LP (0.988, 0.958), then July. I did not investigate why.\n# - **Level:** the true weekly on-share alone gives 0.981, and the blend's weekly level alone 0.973, so the level still has room.\n# - **Calibration:** close to the diagonal, slightly overconfident at low probabilities (predicted 0.05, observed about 0.11). The metric uses only ranks, so I did not recalibrate."
RUN_TABLE="Times in minutes, each run from an empty directory with 4 cores and a 32 GB limit enforced by Slurm.\n#\n# | Run | Hardware | Total | LP | XGBoost | ST-Net | Peak RAM (GiB) | ST-Net GPU (GiB) | Against submitted file |\n# |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n# | Notebook 1 | H100 | 40 | 37 | 0.4 | 2.3 | 16.8 | 3.3 | Byte-identical |\n# | Notebook 2 | H100 | 40 | 37 | 0.4 | 2.3 | 16.7 | 3.2 | Byte-identical |\n# | Notebook 1 | P100, XGBoost on CPU | 307 | 62 | 7.1 | 238 | 17.7 | 4.7 | Spearman 0.99908 |\n# | Notebook 1 | No GPU, before the fallback code | 171 | 37 | 6.3 | 128 | 17.6 | - | Spearman 0.99908 |"
CPU="The slowest run took 5 h 7 min, and no run used more than 17.7 GiB. Without a GPU, notebook 1 took 171 minutes. The largest logit difference to the submitted file is 1.26 and the 99th percentile 0.48. Notebook 2 uses the same code but was not run this way."
GPU="XGBoost 3.4.1 does not support the Tesla P100 (`not compiled for SM 60`), so it fell back to the CPU there. ST-Net stayed on the P100 and took longer than on 4 CPU cores (238 against 128 minutes). I did not investigate why."
CL="the slowest measured run took 5 h 7 min, a factor of 2.3 below 12 hours. All runs used server CPUs, and a laptop can be slower."
